//! check: fail(E0401)
//! phase: typecheck
//! conforms: std.x.crypto.curve25519, ty.byte
//!
//! **THE CONTRACT MOVED INTO THE TYPE, SO THE WITNESS MOVED WITH IT
//! (sc35).** This file used to pin a RUNTIME trap: the byte currency was
//! `List[int]` (§11's interim, F-0035), a caller could spell an element
//! outside 0..255, and the module's ingestion guard trapped `assert`
//! before the value could be used. s136 (wolf-lang#231) moved the eight
//! byte builtins to `List[byte]` and sc35 renamed std's byte tier onto
//! them, so a `List[byte]` CANNOT HOLD a non-byte and the guard was
//! unreachable code — deleted rather than left as decoration. The
//! contract is not weaker; it is enforced one layer down and one phase
//! earlier. The program below is unchanged in shape and is now REFUSED
//! at `typecheck` with **E0401**, whose note names the fix in the
//! language's own words (`b as int`, or narrow this side with
//! `as byte`). The sc28 precedent is exact: when a discipline becomes
//! static, the DIRECTIVE modernizes and the file loses a name that
//! promised the old outcome (`use_after_final_trap.lu` ->
//! `use_after_final.lu`, §13's name-promise).
//!
//! **The lupin lane DIVERGES here and the divergence is the point.**
//! lupin 0.1.23 is dynamically typed and has no `byte`, so it executes
//! this program to its honest end where both compilers refuse it. That
//! is `divergent(exit(…))` in the ledger — F-0098's word, used for
//! exactly this shape (a static rejection on the compiler lanes against
//! an honest dynamic run on the interpreter) — and it retires the day
//! is36 ships a lupin that carries `[type.byte]`. See F-0108.

use std.x.crypto.curve25519

fn main() -> int {
    var scalar = List[byte]()
    var i: int = 0
    while i < 31 {
        (mut scalar).push(0)
        i = i + 1
    }
    (mut scalar).push(256)
    let u = "a 32-byte curve25519 u-coord!!!!".bytes()
    print("reached the call")
    let out = curve25519.x25519(scalar, u) else List[byte]()
    print("unreachable: {out.len}")
    0
}
